>
>
> Jessica
>
>
>
> As Tiago initially described, it makes sense that an abundance estimate from the half normal detection function will likely be smaller than an estimate using the hazard rate detection function for these data because of the large number of detections at small distances.
>
>
>
> The hazard rate fits that spike, resulting in a detection probability within the covered region that is quite small. This small estimated detection probability then presumes that for every whale you detect, there are several that you do not detect. The half normal model presumes the detection function falls off more slowing, resulting in a larger detection probability—the consequence of the larger detection probability being that for each whale you detected there was a smaller number of undetected whales.
>
>
>
> "Confidence" in your result rests with your sentiment about the large number of detections between 0 and 200m, relative to the smaller number of detections in the next interval 200-400m. Do you think detectability falls off as rapidly as the hazard rate model predicts, or more slowly as the half normal model predicts.  This is the reason we advise survey crews to avoid "guarding the trackline" because data sets with large numbers of detections at small distances (relative to other distances) create these analytical challenges.
>
>
>
